# 3. WORDPRESS INTEGRATION ARCHITECTURE — PLAN AHEAD

The implementation will later be integrated into WordPress.

Build with the following future workflow in mind:

**Existing React application → Production build → JavaScript and CSS assets → WordPress page integration.**

The new course page and homepage section should be independently mountable.

### Required implementation structure

The preferred architecture is:

- A reusable course data source.
- A reusable featured course component.
- A dedicated course page component.
- A homepage courses section component.
- A shared styling layer.
- Independent mount points or entry points for WordPress integration.

Use the existing project architecture wherever possible.

### WordPress compatibility requirements

**A. Independent mount points**

Prepare the two components so they can be mounted independently.

Use the following proposed mount identifiers unless the existing project already has an established naming convention:

Homepage:

`#cg-courses-section`

Course page:

`#cg-diploma-ai-course`

The components must not depend on each other being present on the same page.

The homepage section must render without the course page.

The course page must render without the homepage section.

**B. CSS isolation**

This is particularly important.

WordPress themes, Elementor and other plugins may introduce CSS that conflicts with React components.

Therefore:

- Scope all new component styles under unique parent classes.
- Do not introduce unnecessary global selectors.
- Avoid modifying `body`, `html`, `h1`, `h2`, `p`, `a`, `button` or other global styles.
- Avoid overriding existing WordPress theme styles.
- Do not introduce broad CSS resets.
- Do not assume WordPress and React share the same CSS environment.
- Keep all component-specific styling inside the new feature's CSS namespace.

Suggested namespaces:

`.cg-courses-section`

`.cg-diploma-ai-course`

Use CSS variables only within the appropriate scoped namespace when possible.

**C. JavaScript isolation**

- Avoid unnecessary global variables.
- Avoid modifying existing website JavaScript.
- Avoid conflicting event listeners.
- Avoid duplicate React root creation.
- Check whether the target mount element exists before mounting.
- Ensure the components initialise safely if the corresponding mount element is absent.
- Avoid executing the same initialisation multiple times.
- Ensure the components do not interfere with Elementor, Gravity Forms or existing WordPress scripts.

**D. Build compatibility**

Inspect the existing Vite configuration and build scripts.

Preserve the existing application configuration.

Do not introduce an additional build system.

Prepare the implementation so that the homepage section and dedicated course page can be included in the future WordPress JavaScript/CSS integration.

If the current project uses a single application bundle, determine whether the existing architecture supports independent mount points or whether separate entry points are more appropriate.

Prefer the least disruptive approach.

Do not duplicate dependencies or bundle unnecessary application features.

**E. Future WordPress integration**

Do not create or modify WordPress PHP snippets, plugins, Elementor widgets or WordPress pages at this stage.

Instead, prepare the React implementation and provide:

- Exact mount identifiers.
- JavaScript build output.
- CSS build output.
- Required asset dependencies.
- Mounting instructions.
- Any required WordPress integration considerations.

The goal is to make the subsequent WordPress integration straightforward.

**Important:** The final implementation must remain compatible with the existing WordPress integration workflow, not introduce a new deployment architecture.

